    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>For many renters, the “landlord special” of cracked beige ceramic, dated terracotta or scuffed lino is the most discouraging part of a new home. Because your deposit is at stake, permanent work such as ripping up tile or drilling into the subfloor is off the table. Living with a floor that drains the energy from your space isn’t your only option, though.</p>
<p>There is now a wide choice of renter-friendly products designed to give a smarter look without screws, nails or permanent adhesive. From peel-and-stick vinyl to floating floors, you can transform a kitchen, bathroom or living area. This guide explores the most effective drill-free ways to hide ugly tile, along with the honest limits of each.</p>
<p>Before you start, read your tenancy agreement: some say no adhesive products on floors, walls or fixtures. If in doubt, ask your landlord or letting agent and get their answer in writing. Photograph the tiles alongside your check-in inventory, and test any adhesive product on a hidden tile, such as behind the toilet or under a unit, for a week or two of normal use before covering a whole floor. Adhesive can lift old glazes, loose grout or painted finishes, so check the test patch carefully when you peel it off.</p>

<h2 id="vinyl-decals">1. Vinyl decals</h2>
<p>Peel-and-stick vinyl decals are the most accessible and affordable way to update a tiled floor, including in kitchens and bathrooms. They are essentially hard-wearing, water-resistant stickers made to fit standard tile sizes, usually 10 × 10, 15 × 15 or 30 × 30 cm (4, 6 or 12 in).</p>
<p>Their main advantage is the ability to introduce intricate patterns, such as Moroccan, Mediterranean or Victorian styles, without the work of traditional tiling. They are thin enough not to create a noticeable height difference, so doors shouldn’t catch on the floor. For a neat finish, degrease the existing tile thoroughly: any leftover soap scum or dust will stop the adhesive bonding. Decals cut slightly smaller than the tile, sitting inside the grout lines, stick to the smooth glaze rather than porous grout and are easier to remove later.</p>
<p>When it’s time to move out, warming the decals with a hairdryer on a low setting softens the glue so they can be peeled away. Most come off cleanly from sound, glazed tile, but results vary with the product, the age of the tile and how long the decals have been down, which is why the hidden-tile test matters.</p>

<h2 id="vinyl-sheets">2. Vinyl sheets</h2>
<p>If you prefer a seamless look rather than a tiled pattern, luxury vinyl sheet (LVS) is an excellent loose-lay option. Modern vinyl sheet is a far cry from the plastic-looking rolls of the past, with realistic wood grains, slate textures and matt finishes.</p>
<p>In a rental, avoid gluing the whole sheet down. A common approach is to lay it loose, cut to fit tightly around the toilet and units, and secure it only around the edges and at any seams. Be careful with double-sided carpet tape: it can leave residue or damage some tile glazes and floor finishes. Test it first on a hidden spot and choose a tape designed to be removable. The weight of the sheet and your furniture helps keep the centre stable.</p>
<p>Vinyl sheet works well over large areas of cracked or uneven tile because it bridges small gaps and gives a smoother surface, although very uneven tiles or deep grout lines may still show through over time. It is durable and easy to mop, which suits busy households with pets.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Measure the full floor and leave a 1 cm (⅜ in) gap around the edge when cutting loose-lay vinyl. Floors and walls are rarely square, and a small gap stops the sheet buckling as the room warms and cools.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="click-lock-planks">3. Click-lock planks</h2>
<p>For a more substantial feel that mimics real wood or stone, click-lock luxury vinyl planks (LVP) are a popular choice. They need no adhesive or fixings: a tongue-and-groove system locks the planks together so the floor “floats” over the existing tile.</p>
<p>Because they are thicker than decals or sheet (usually 4 mm to 7 mm), they add some sound insulation and a more comfortable feel underfoot. If your landlord’s tile is particularly cold or hard, LVP adds a welcome layer between you and it. Floating floors need a reasonably flat surface, so check the maker’s guidance on how much unevenness the product tolerates, and leave the expansion gap they specify around the edges of the room. If you’re covering a bathroom or kitchen, check the product is suitable for wet rooms.</p>
<p>The main consideration is height. Measure the clearance under your doors before you buy. Don’t remove or plane doors yourself: trimming a door is a change to the property, so ask your landlord first and get their permission in writing. Many standard planks fit under a typical door gap without any changes, but it’s worth checking every door the floor passes.</p>

<h2 id="area-rugs">4. Area rugs</h2>
<p>Never underestimate the power of layering. If you’re on a tight budget or a short let, covering most of the floor with large rugs is the fastest fix. To make it look intentional rather than like a cover-up, layer them.</p>
<p>Start with a large, neutral base rug in a natural fibre such as jute or sisal that spans almost the full width of the room. Then place a smaller, more decorative rug on top in the seating or dining area. This adds texture, warmth and softens sound. To stop rugs sliding on slick tile or becoming a trip hazard, use a felt or rug pad suitable for your floor. Be aware that some rubber or latex-backed rugs and mats can discolour certain floor finishes over time, so test in a corner, and lift and air rugs regularly so moisture doesn’t get trapped underneath.</p>

<h2 id="floor-cloths">5. Floor cloths</h2>
<p>Floor cloths are a traditional decorating idea that has seen a modern revival. They are heavy canvas sheets treated with several layers of paint and varnish, which makes them hard-wearing, water-resistant and able to lie flat against the floor.</p>
<p>Because they are a stiffened fabric, they are thinner than most rugs and less likely to catch a foot in busy areas such as hallways and kitchens, though a non-slip underlay is still sensible on smooth tile. They’re ideal for covering specific problem zones where tiles are particularly stained or ugly. Floor cloths are also highly customisable; many makers paint them to look like encaustic tiles or abstract art. When you move, they can be rolled up (painted side out, to avoid cracking) and taken to your next home.</p>

<h2 id="interlocking-mats">6. Interlocking mats</h2>
<p>In utility spaces, home gyms or playrooms, interlocking foam or rubber mats are a practical, low-risk option because they sit on the floor without adhesive. Beyond primary-coloured alphabet mats for children, there are now versions that mimic wood grain, marble or patterned designs.</p>
<p>They are easy to install, snapping together like puzzle pieces, and add cushioning over cold, hard tile. In a home office, they help protect the landlord’s tile from the constant rolling of desk chair wheels. Because they’re modular, you can fit them into awkward cupboards or laundry nooks without specialist tools. As with rubber-backed rugs, check that the mat material won’t mark your floor finish, and lift them occasionally to let the floor beneath dry.</p>

<h2 id="preparation-and-removal">Preparation and removal</h2>
<p>The success of any no-drill floor cover depends on preparation. Before fitting any of the options above, deep-clean the existing tile with a cleaner that leaves no residue, rinse and let it dry fully. If the grout lines are deep and you’re using thin vinyl, accept that a slight texture may show through; filling grout lines is more of a change to the property, so ask permission before using any filler.</p>
<p>When the time comes to move out, give yourself a few days and be patient:</p>
<ul>
<li>Warm adhesive products with a hairdryer on a low setting and peel slowly at a low angle, folding the cover back on itself.</li>
<li>Remove leftover residue with warm soapy water first, then, if needed, an adhesive remover suitable for tiles and grout, tested on a hidden spot first.</li>
<li>For floating floors and mats, simply take them apart, stack them and take them to your next home, which makes them more of an investment than a temporary fix.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Assess the traffic.</strong> Choose thin decals for low-traffic bathrooms, but opt for click-lock planks or heavier rugs for busy living spaces.</li>
<li><strong>Measure twice.</strong> Allow around 10% extra material for vinyl products to account for mistakes and corner cuts.</li>
<li><strong>Check clearances.</strong> Measure the gap under your doors before buying thick LVP or rugs to avoid doors catching.</li>
<li><strong>Clean thoroughly.</strong> Adhesives only stick to a surface free of wax, grease and dust.</li>
<li><strong>Test and ask first.</strong> Patch test on a hidden tile and get written permission from your landlord where in doubt.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the scraps.</strong> Save leftover planks or decals in case you need to replace a damaged section during your tenancy.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="damage-original-grout">Will these methods damage the original grout?</h3>
<p>Not usually, but it’s possible. Peel-and-stick products are designed for smooth, non-porous surfaces. If the grout is old, crumbling or unsealed, adhesive can pull up loose bits, and it can also lift worn glazes or painted tiles. Test on a hidden spot first, and warm the adhesive during removal to reduce the risk.</p>
<h3 id="use-in-the-shower">Can I use these in the shower?</h3>
<p>No. Many vinyl products are water-resistant, but they aren’t designed to be soaked or constantly sprayed. Water trapped behind them can lead to mould. Use them on the bathroom floor, away from the shower tray itself.</p>
<h3 id="click-lock-in-a-rental">Is click-lock flooring really okay for a rental?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes. Because it’s a floating floor, it doesn’t attach to the subfloor or the walls; it stays in place through its own weight and the locking system. Don’t nail anything into the floor or skirting. If you want to hide the expansion gap, use beading fixed to the skirting with removable adhesive strips, tested first, and check with your landlord before fitting it.</p>
<h3 id="cleaning-temporary-floor">How do I clean my new temporary floor?</h3>
<p>For vinyl products, use a damp mop with a mild, pH-neutral cleaner. Avoid excessive water, as it can seep into seams and sit on the landlord’s original tile, which may lead to mould if it doesn’t dry.</p>
<h3 id="most-budget-friendly">Which option is the most budget-friendly?</h3>
<p>Large rugs or basic vinyl decals are usually the most affordable. Click-lock planks cost more but are durable and can be reused in your next home, which can make them better value over time.</p>

